#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""deliver-library-credentials — hand the Library's mints to the accounts that use them.

    sudo bin/deliver-library-credentials               deliver what is missing
    sudo bin/deliver-library-credentials --dry-run      say what would happen, change nothing
    sudo bin/deliver-library-credentials --check        report only; 1 if anything is still undelivered
    sudo bin/deliver-library-credentials --status       the same report, changing nothing

**THE PROBLEM THIS CLOSES.** On the two- and three-account shapes, company
decision 0025 point 5 runs the Library as its own service account —
`_library` on macOS, `library` on Linux — with a home at mode 700 that no
other account can read. `jobs/library.toml`'s own `prepare` steps mint
Orient's three Library credentials, the credential `agentctl review-post`
posts its records with, the credentials that read and write the crew
registry, and `mint-crew-ops-credentials`'s own pair for every registered
crew, all under that account's `~/.orient-control`. Each one is a credential
for a specific OTHER account to present TO the Library, never one the
Library reads back, so once minted it has to reach the account that
actually uses it. Before this existed, nothing copied any of them anywhere,
and every one of those would find a path nothing had populated.

**WHAT IT DOES.** Every regular file directly under the Library account's
`~/.orient-control` is checked against `is_known_credential` below. A file
this does not recognise is reported, never delivered — see "WHAT IT NEVER
DOES". A file it recognises is copied to each destination account's own
`~/.orient-control`, mode 600, owned by that account, UNLESS it already
holds a copy with the exact same bytes, in which case nothing is written.
**This is a content comparison, not an existence check**: `jobs/library.toml`'s
own `prepare` replaces Orient's `library-token` once, with the
`registry.read` grant a credential minted before that grant existed lacks,
and a destination still holding the pre-grant copy needs the replacement
delivered just as much as holding nothing at all. The Library's own copy is
the authority for every credential this delivers, including on a later
rotation.

**ORIENT'S OWN THREE CREDENTIALS GO TO ORIENT'S OWN ACCOUNT, NEVER `agents`.**
`library-token`, `library-client-token` and `capture-token`
(`ORIENT_CREDENTIAL_NAMES`) are the only three presented from Orient's own
account: the first two read directly by Orient's own server code, the third
by `bin/orient-deploy`'s own child dropped to Orient's account to post a
`deploy_failure` signal (root itself opens no Library credential). Once
Orient runs as its own service account it has no path into any agents home
at all — the same reason its database and its other credentials move with
it. `main` resolves Orient's own account (`orient_account_name`) the same
way it resolves the Library's own, and refuses the same way when the
Library's account exists but Orient's does not. These three are split out of
`plan_delivery`'s own `known` before `route_known` ever sees it
(`plan_and_route`), and delivered to Orient's one account the same way every
other credential reaches its destination: a child dropped to Orient's own
account, read for comparison and written when it differs. Orient is one
account regardless of how many entities this install declares, so none of
the three is ever routed by entity, the way a crew's own pair is below.

**EVERY OTHER ENTITY-AGNOSTIC CREDENTIAL GOES TO `agents`, OR TO EVERY
ENTITY'S OWN AGENTS ACCOUNT ON A MULTI-ENTITY INSTALL; A CREW'S OWN PAIR GOES
ONLY TO ITS OWN ENTITY.** `--install-json` (default `/opt/builder/install.json`)
names the same record `provision-accounts` and `install-privileged` read
`entities` from: a single entity's destination is the bare `agents` every
install used before entities existed, and with more than one it is every
entity's own `<entity>-agents`. Every name in `KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES` is
entity-agnostic infrastructure and reaches every one of those destinations,
for the reasons in that name's own comment. A crew's own `ops-<crew>-token`
and `ops-co-<crew>-token` (`CREW_OPS_RE`) are different: `payload/skills/
new-crew/SKILL.md` has `agentctl crew register` take `--entity`, naming
which entity's agents account runs that crew's own sessions and dispatched
runs, and on a multi-entity install a crew's pair reaches only that one —
see `crew_entity_map`, `route_known` and `crew_candidates_for_ops_name`. A
crew whose own registry row names no entity, or names one this install
does not declare, gets its pair delivered to nobody, and so does a file
name two registered crews could own (a crew `<x>` and a crew `co-<x>` both
read `ops-co-<x>-token`, whichever entities they belong to); `main`
reports either on stderr. A shared name is also removed from every
destination that already holds a copy (`shared_ops_names`,
`_revoke_in_child`), since a copy delivered before the second crew
registered may hold either crew's credential. A real run (`--dry-run` included) still delivers everything
else and exits 0 for it — the next run reads the same registry row and
reports it again, since nothing here remembers that a name was orphaned —
and `--check` still exits 1 for it, since that mode's whole job is to say
work is left. Guessing a destination or broadcasting to every entity is
never done. The single-entity install needs no entity routing, since
there is exactly one destination, and every recognised credential reaches
it except a shared name. A shared name is withheld and revoked there too:
both colliding crews run as the one agents account, so the account
boundary does not separate them, but the file's content carries one
crew's grant, the `item.create` grant included, and the other crew's
sessions and dispatched runs would read it as their own. There is no other reader to route an
entity-agnostic credential to: `agentctl review-post` and every job or tool
that posts a signal read their credential through `signal_client.TOKEN_FILE`,
which resolves to `~/.orient-control/agent-platform-token` under whatever
account `HOME` names — an entity's own agents account, since dispatch and a
crew's own session both run as it. The root-owned launcher that posts a
review's verdict (`review_launch.py`'s own `post_comment`/`post_check`) uses
its own token, reads no file under `.orient-control` and has no
`mcp-kb-server`/`mcp-ops-server` config naming one — `bin/install-privileged`
ships only those two tools to a `reviewers` account
(`REVIEWER_BINS`), and neither is `agentctl`. So a `reviewers` account,
wherever one exists, has no reader for any file this recognises, and
nothing here ever addresses it.

**WHY THIS RUNS AS ROOT, AND WHY IT NEVER READS OR WRITES AS ROOT ON THE
`agents` SIDE.** The Library's home is unreadable to every other account,
and `agents`' home is not writable by the Library's account either, so root
is the only account that can read one side and reach the other at all. But
root reading or writing directly by path under `agents`' own home trusts
that account's own directory and file names, which that account controls —
a name root opens there can be a symlink `agents` planted, or, for a
destination it reads for comparison rather than follows as a symlink, a
FIFO with no writer, which a plain blocking open hangs on forever. So root
only reads the Library's own copies, with `os.O_NOFOLLOW`; everything on the
`agents` side — reading what is already there to compare, and writing what
differs — happens in a forked child that has already dropped to `agents`'
own uid and gid, so neither carries any right root has and `agents` does
not. `bin/stand-up-backend` runs this step right after `supervise`, which is
the step that installs `jobs/library.toml` and runs its `prepare` — the
mints this delivers.

**IT ALSO MINTS WHAT `jobs/library.toml`'S OWN `prepare` CANNOT MINT AGAIN.**
A crew registered after the install has no ops-credential pair: the
`new-crew` skill's own `mint-crew-ops-credentials` call runs as the account
a crew's own session runs as, which cannot open the Library's database to
read the registry, on the shapes this delivers on. Before a real run copies
anything, it runs that same command once more, as the Library's own
account, so a crew registered since the last run gets its pair minted here
and delivered in the same step. A failure there is reported and does not
block delivering what is already minted.

**ONLY ON THE SHAPES WHERE THE LIBRARY HAS ITS OWN ACCOUNT.** The
single-account shape runs the Library as the operator, the same account as
everything else, so there is nothing to deliver and this reports that and
changes nothing. An install that has not yet provisioned the Library's own
account — before company decision 0025 point 5, or the two-/three-account
shape mid-upgrade — gets the same answer, for the same reason: nothing has
been minted anywhere this has not already looked. Orient's own account is
provisioned on the same shapes and at the same step as the Library's
(`provision-accounts`), so this never finds the Library's account present
without Orient's: a machine where that has somehow happened refuses by name
instead, the same way it already refuses a Library account with no `agents`.

**ON THE THREE-ACCOUNT SHAPE, IT ALSO PLACES THE REGISTRY READER TOKEN AT A
FIXED ROOT PATH.** agent-platform's `review_launch.py` runs as root, by the one
sudo rule `install-privileged`'s three-account root half places, and reads the
crew registry with the credential at a fixed path
(`REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_DIR`/`REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_NAME`, `/opt/builder/etc/
registry-reader-token` by default) — nobody's `.orient-control`, since the
launcher runs as root, never as any entity's agents or reviewers account. This
delivers the Library's own `agent-platform-registry-reader-token` there too,
the exact same bytes every entity's agents account already receives, whenever
the Library holds a copy the root path does not already hold byte-for-byte.
`read_install_shape` reads the `shape` field `install-privileged` wrote to
`--install-json`, the same field `bin/uninstall-builder`'s own
`read_install_shape` reads; the single-account and two-account shapes have no
`reviewers` account and no launcher to serve, so this never touches that path
on either. The destination directory is never created here —
`install-privileged`'s own `install_three` already creates it, mode 700,
before this ever runs — and a missing or wrongly-owned directory is refused
rather than made.

**REFUSES BEFORE READING OR WRITING ANYTHING, WHEN A HOME IS NOT THE
ACCOUNT'S OWN.** `bin/uninstall-builder`'s `library_removal_plan` makes the
same check before it deletes under the Library's home; this makes it before
it writes under `agents`' or Orient's, for the same reason — a stale or
misconfigured directory-service entry must not hand a real file to, or read
one from, whatever that entry actually points at. The Library's own `.orient-control`
gets a stronger version of the same refusal: `plan_delivery` opens it once,
with `O_NOFOLLOW`, and checks the resulting file descriptor's own owner with
`fstat` before listing or reading a single entry through that same
descriptor — never a second, separate lookup of its name, which the Library's
own account (owning its entire home at mode 700) could otherwise swap for a
symlink in the gap between a check and a later read. `_read_no_follow`
closes the same gap one entry at a time: it opens with `O_NONBLOCK` and
confirms a regular file with `fstat` before trusting the content, the same
guard `_read_dest` already makes on the `agents` side, so swapping one
entry's name for a FIFO in the gap between the directory listing and this
read cannot hang this root-run tool either.

**WHAT IT NEVER DOES.** It never delivers a file whose name it does not
recognise — `KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES`'s own comment says why a name is
correct where it is rather than guessed from its shape. It never reads or
writes a destination in another account's home by path as root; every such
read or write happens in the dropped child. The one destination root writes
itself is `/opt/builder/etc/registry-reader-token` on the three-account
install, inside a directory it first confirms is root-owned with mode 0700
(`require_registry_reader_dir`), so no other account can reach that path. It never deletes anything, on either side. It never
reads the Library's own database as root either — `crew_entity_map` reads
it the same way `mint_missing_crew_credentials` mints into `.orient-control`:
in a child already dropped to the Library account's own uid and gid.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import sqlite3
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import pwd
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent


def _load_sibling(module_name: str, filename: str):
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(module_name, str(HERE / filename))
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader(module_name, loader))
    sys.modules[module_name] = module
    loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


# Which entities this install names, and the account pair each gets: the same
# module `provision-accounts`, `install-privileged` and `uninstall-builder`
# already load as a sibling, so none of them can disagree about where the
# list comes from or what an entity's agents account is called.
ae = _load_sibling("account_entities", "account_entities.py")

PROG = "deliver-library-credentials"

# Duplicated from `bin/provision-accounts`' own `LIBRARY_ACCOUNT_NAMES`
# rather than imported, the same way `bin/stand-up-backend` already
# duplicates it (company decision 0025 point 5 names the convention).
LIBRARY_ACCOUNT_NAMES = {"macos": "_library", "linux": "library"}
# Duplicated from `bin/provision-accounts`' own `ORIENT_ACCOUNT_NAMES` the
# same way, rather than imported: Orient's own single destination for
# `ORIENT_CREDENTIAL_NAMES` below, never one of `destination_accounts`' own
# per-entity agents accounts.
ORIENT_ACCOUNT_NAMES = {"macos": "_orient", "linux": "orient"}
ORIENT_CONTROL = ".orient-control"

# Every file `jobs/library.toml`'s own `prepare` can mint directly under the
# Library account's `~/.orient-control`, other than Orient's own three below,
# the per-crew ops pair (`CREW_OPS_RE` below) and the idempotency marker
# (`NON_CREDENTIAL_MARKERS` below). Every one of these is read by `agents`,
# never by any other account — see the module docstring's "EVERY
# ENTITY-AGNOSTIC CREDENTIAL GOES TO `agents`":
#   - `ops-mcp-token`: the work-queue tools' shared bootstrap credential,
#     read by `bin/agentctl` and `lib/agent-platform/signal_client.py` from
#     a crew's own session or a run dispatched into it — both `agents`.
#   - `agent-platform-token`: the credential `signal_client.TOKEN_FILE`
#     resolves to `~/.orient-control/agent-platform-token` under, read by
#     `agentctl review-post` and every job or tool that posts a signal
#     through that client — again `agents`, since dispatch and a crew's own
#     session both run as it. The root-owned review launcher posts a
#     review's own verdict with a different, separately-held token and
#     never reads this file at all.
#   - `agent-platform-registry-reader-token`,
#     `agent-platform-registry-writer-token`: registry reads outside
#     Orient (`lib/agent-platform/projects.py`) and crew registration
#     (`agentctl crew register`, the `new-crew` skill) — both run as a
#     crew's own session, `agents`.
#   - `reference-sync-token`: minted by `jobs/library.toml`'s own
#     `account-prepare` (FORMAT.md, "account-prepare"), which, unlike
#     `prepare`, never runs once `--single-account` has dropped the job's
#     account, so a single-account install holds none. Read by whichever
#     account runs `agentctl kb sync`.
#   - `deck-library-token`: Deck's own approved-client credential, minted by
#     `jobs/library.toml`'s own `prepare` and narrowed in place to
#     `--holds-reserved=`, granted `item.signed_act`, by its own
#     `account-prepare` step, on these two shapes. Deck reads it over ssh as
#     `agents`, the same account every other name in this set already
#     reaches, and the single-account shape needs no delivery at all, since
#     `{home}` already resolves to the operator there.
#   - `kb-person-save-token`: the credential the document service presents
#     to the Library's person-save route on Orient's behalf, minted by
#     `jobs/library.toml`'s own `prepare` on every shape (never gated by
#     `account-prepare`, since the document service runs as `agents` even
#     on the single-account shape). Read from
#     `~/.orient-control/kb-person-save-token` by the Bosun's `agentctl kb
#     apply` — `agents`, since that is the account the document service
#     runs as.
KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES = frozenset({
    "ops-mcp-token",
    "agent-platform-token",
    "agent-platform-registry-reader-token",
    "agent-platform-registry-writer-token",
    "reference-sync-token",
    "deck-library-token",
    "kb-person-save-token",
})

# Orient's own three Library credentials, minted the same way and in the
# same place as `KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES` above, but delivered to Orient's own
# single account (`ORIENT_ACCOUNT_NAMES`) rather than to any entity's agents
# account: Orient runs as its own service account once it has one
# (`jobs/orient.toml`: `account = "orient"`), with no path into any agents
# home at all, so these three can no longer be delivered the way the rest of
# this set is. `capture-token` is read there not by Orient's own server code
# but by a child `bin/orient-deploy` drops to Orient's account to post a
# `deploy_failure` signal — it still belongs on this list rather than
# `KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES` above, because it is Orient's own account that
# presents it, never `agents`'. Orient is one account regardless of how many
# entities this install declares, so unlike a crew's own ops pair, none of
# the three is ever routed by entity — see `plan_and_route` in `main`.
ORIENT_CREDENTIAL_NAMES = frozenset({
    "library-token",
    "library-client-token",
    "capture-token",
})

# `mint-crew-ops-credentials`' own two file templates (its `PAIR`), minted
# directly under the Library account's `~/.orient-control` by the
# `--orient-control` flag `jobs/library.toml`'s `prepare` and
# `mint_missing_crew_credentials` below both pass, for every crew the
# registry carries. Both are read by that crew's own session or a run
# dispatched into it — the entity its registry row names, on a multi-entity
# install; see `crew_candidates_for_ops_name` and `route_known`. The middle
# of the name is constrained the same way `mint-crew-ops-credentials`' own
# `CREW_RE` constrains a crew's own slug (`^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,63}$`),
# captured so that function can look it up. `ops-mcp-token` above happens
# to match this pattern too (crew "mcp"), which `crew_candidates_for_ops_name`
# treats as the entity-agnostic name it already is in
# `KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES` — checked first, so this pattern never routes it
# by a "mcp" crew; the mint side of that same collision (a crew literally
# NAMED "mcp" would otherwise mint or reuse this exact file for itself) is
# refused by name in `payload/bin/mint-crew-ops-credentials`'s own
# `RESERVED_CREW_NAMES`.
#
# A name beginning `ops-co-` is separately ambiguous about its OWN crew:
# this pattern alone cannot tell "the item.create pair for the crew named
# by what follows `co-`" apart from "the plain pair for a crew whose own
# name starts with `co-`" — a crew named `co-widget` mints its own PLAIN
# pair at exactly the file name `widget`'s item.create pair would use
# (`ops-co-widget-token`). `crew_candidates_for_ops_name` returns both
# readings; `route_known` resolves between them against the registry
# rather than guessing.
CREW_OPS_RE = re.compile(r"^ops-([a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,63})-token$")

# Regular files directly under the Library account's `~/.orient-control`
# that are not credentials at all: `jobs/library.toml`'s own idempotency
# markers for its one-time re-mints, of `library-token` (`--replace --grants
# registry.read`) and of Orient's `library-client-token` (`--replace --grants
# signal.post_draft`). Nothing reads them; none is ever delivered or reported
# as a name this does not recognise.
NON_CREDENTIAL_MARKERS = {
    "library-token-registry-read-granted",
    "library-client-token-post-draft-granted",
}

# `payload/bin/mint-crew-ops-credentials`, resolved relative to this file
# rather than to any account's deploy area: the copy this runs is whichever
# this script's own install extracted beside it — the same copy
# `bin/stand-up-backend` already runs this and `install-jobs` from (see the
# module docstring's "WHY THIS RUNS AS ROOT"), so there is no deploy area to
# resolve and no account's `PATH` to trust.
DEFAULT_CREW_MINT_SCRIPT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "payload" / "bin" / "mint-crew-ops-credentials"

# Where the review launcher's own copy of `agent-platform-registry-reader-token`
# goes on the three-account shape — never under any account's `.orient-control`,
# since `review_launch.py` runs as root. A plain module constant, like
# `DEFAULT_CREW_MINT_SCRIPT` above, rather than a flag or an environment
# variable: a test overrides it the same way, by assigning the module
# attribute directly (see `tools/test_deliver_library_credentials.py`'s own
# `World`).
REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_DIR = "/opt/builder/etc"
REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_NAME = "registry-reader-token"

# Owner of the file at `REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_DIR`/`REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_NAME`:
# gid 0 is `wheel` on macOS and `root` on Linux, the same numeric group
# either way. Tests, which do not run as root, set both to their own ids —
# the same pattern `bin/github-gate`'s own `ROOT_UID`/`ROOT_GID` already use.
ROOT_UID = 0
ROOT_GID = 0


class Refused(Exception):
    """Something the operator has to decide about. Never a stack trace."""


class _Unreadable:
    """Returned by `_read_no_follow` and `_read_dest` for a path that exists
    but could not be trusted as a regular file's content. Never equal to any
    bytes, including another `_Unreadable` instance's — the content behind
    it is unknown, so it is never treated as matching anything, including
    itself."""

    def __eq__(self, other):
        return False

    def __ne__(self, other):
        return True


UNREADABLE = _Unreadable()


def library_account_name(platform: str | None = None) -> str:
    platform = platform or ("macos" if sys.platform == "darwin" else "linux")
    return LIBRARY_ACCOUNT_NAMES[platform]


def orient_account_name(platform: str | None = None) -> str:
    platform = platform or ("macos" if sys.platform == "darwin" else "linux")
    return ORIENT_ACCOUNT_NAMES[platform]


def account_home(name: str) -> str | None:
    try:
        return pwd.getpwnam(name).pw_dir
    except KeyError:
        return None


def account_ids(name: str) -> tuple[int, int] | None:
    try:
        entry = pwd.getpwnam(name)
    except KeyError:
        return None
    return entry.pw_uid, entry.pw_gid


def owned_by(path: str, uid: int) -> bool:
    """Whether `path` exists, is a real directory and is owned by `uid`.

    Never follows a symlink: a symlink at an account's home or at
    `.orient-control` would otherwise read or write through it to wherever
    it points.
    """
    try:
        info = os.lstat(path)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return True  # nothing there yet is not foreign; it just does not exist
    if stat.S_ISLNK(info.st_mode):
        return False
    return info.st_uid == uid


def _read_no_follow(name: str, dir_fd: int) -> bytes | None | _Unreadable:
    """`name`'s content, opened relative to the already-open `dir_fd` —
    never by a second lookup of the directory's own path — and never
    following a symlink at `name` itself. `None` for "does not exist",
    `UNREADABLE` for "exists but is a symlink, is not a regular file, or
    could not be opened" — the caller treats both of the latter as not
    matching whatever it is comparing against, never as empty.

    Opened with `O_NONBLOCK` as well as `O_NOFOLLOW`, the same guard
    `_read_dest` makes on the `agents` side: `plan_delivery` lists this
    entry and this call reopens it by name a moment later, both relative to
    the same already-open directory descriptor, and the Library's own
    account (owning that directory) can unlink a regular file and `mkfifo`
    a replacement in the gap between the two. A plain blocking open of a
    FIFO with no writer hangs forever; `O_NONBLOCK` makes the open itself
    return at once regardless, and the `fstat` right after refuses to trust
    anything but a regular file's content.

    Used only for entries under the Library's own `source_dir` — root's own
    side, which `plan_delivery` reads through one open directory descriptor
    for the whole call, so nothing can be swapped in between the ownership
    check that opened it and this read. Never used for a destination under
    an entity's agents home; see `_read_dest`."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(name, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_NONBLOCK, dir_fd=dir_fd)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    except OSError:
        return UNREADABLE
    try:
        if not stat.S_ISREG(os.fstat(fd).st_mode):
            os.close(fd)
            return UNREADABLE
    except OSError:
        os.close(fd)
        return UNREADABLE
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "rb") as handle:
            return handle.read()
    except OSError:
        return UNREADABLE


def _read_dest(path: Path) -> bytes | None | _Unreadable:
    """`path`'s content, read as whichever account this runs as — called
    from inside a child already dropped to the reader account's own uid and
    gid, and as root only for `/opt/builder/etc/registry-reader-token`,
    whose directory root has just confirmed is its own with mode 0700.

    Opened with `O_NONBLOCK` as well as `O_NOFOLLOW`: `agents` can plant a
    FIFO at a destination name, and a plain blocking open of one with no
    writer hangs forever. `O_NONBLOCK` makes the open itself return at once
    regardless, and the `fstat` right after refuses to trust anything but a
    regular file's content — a FIFO, a device or any other non-regular file
    counts as `UNREADABLE`, the same as a symlink, rather than being read.
    """
    try:
        fd = os.open(str(path), os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_NONBLOCK)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    except OSError:
        return UNREADABLE
    try:
        if not stat.S_ISREG(os.fstat(fd).st_mode):
            os.close(fd)
            return UNREADABLE
    except OSError:
        os.close(fd)
        return UNREADABLE
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "rb") as handle:
            return handle.read()
    except OSError:
        return UNREADABLE


def is_known_credential(name: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `name` is a credential this recognises — one of
    `KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES`, one of `ORIENT_CREDENTIAL_NAMES`, or a crew's
    own ops-pair file (`CREW_OPS_RE`). Every one of these is read by some
    account; see `crew_candidates_for_ops_name` and the module docstring for
    which one."""
    return (name in KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES or name in ORIENT_CREDENTIAL_NAMES
            or bool(CREW_OPS_RE.match(name)))


def crew_candidates_for_ops_name(name: str) -> list[str]:
    """Every crew slug `name` could belong to, as a crew-scoped
    ops-credential's own file name — `[]` when `name` is not one of those
    at all, or is one of `KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES`'s own entity-agnostic
    names even though it happens to match the same shape (`ops-mcp-token`;
    see `CREW_OPS_RE`'s own comment) — checked first, so membership there
    always wins. `route_known` routes every other name unconditionally to
    every destination; only a name this returns candidates for is routed by
    a crew's own entity.

    A name beginning `ops-co-` names two different candidates, because
    `CREW_OPS_RE` cannot tell which of two readings is the real one: the
    crew named by what follows `co-` (whose `item.create` pair this file
    would be), or a crew whose own name happens to start with `co-` (whose
    PLAIN pair this file would be instead). Both are returned — the `co-`
    read as the other pair member's own marker first, then read as part of
    the crew's own name — and `route_known` resolves between them against
    the registry. Any other name returns exactly one candidate."""
    if name in KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES:
        return []
    match = CREW_OPS_RE.match(name)
    if match is None:
        return []
    middle = match.group(1)
    candidates = [middle]
    if middle.startswith("co-") and len(middle) > len("co-"):
        candidates.insert(0, middle[len("co-"):])
    return candidates


DIR_OPEN_FLAGS = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_DIRECTORY


def plan_delivery(source_dir: Path, uid: int) -> tuple[list[tuple[str, bytes]], list[str]]:
    """Every recognised, regular, non-symlink file directly under
    `source_dir`, read here, as root — opened exactly ONCE, as a directory
    file descriptor confirmed by `fstat` to be owned by `uid`, with every
    listing and read done through that same descriptor rather than a
    second, separate lookup of `source_dir`'s own name. The Library's own
    account owns its entire home (mode 700) and can replace
    `.orient-control` with a symlink at any time; opening once and reading
    everything through the resulting descriptor is what makes that
    irrelevant once this call has started — there is no gap between a
    check and a later read for such a swap to land in.

    Returns `(known, unknown)`. `known` is `(name, data)` for every
    recognised name; `unknown` is the names this does not recognise at all.
    A known non-credential marker (`NON_CREDENTIAL_MARKERS`) is silently
    skipped: it is neither known nor unknown.

    Nothing there yet is not foreign, just absent — returns `([], [])`
    rather than raising. Raises `Refused` when something is there but is
    not a plain directory owned by `uid`, a symlink included (`O_NOFOLLOW`
    fails the open itself on one, with `ELOOP`).

    **This never looks at any destination.** Root reads only the Library's
    own side here; whether a destination already holds a matching copy is
    decided afterward, inside a child dropped to that account's own uid and
    gid — see `pending_against_dest`.
    """
    try:
        fd = os.open(str(source_dir), DIR_OPEN_FLAGS)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return [], []
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(
            f"{source_dir} could not be opened as a plain directory ({exc}). Something else "
            f"may have made it; this will not read from it. Nothing was delivered.")
    try:
        info = os.fstat(fd)
        if info.st_uid != uid:
            raise Refused(
                f"{source_dir} is owned by uid {info.st_uid}, not its own account. Something "
                f"else made it; this will not read from it. Nothing was delivered.")
        known: list[tuple[str, bytes]] = []
        unknown: list[str] = []
        for name in sorted(os.listdir(fd)):
            entry_info = os.stat(name, dir_fd=fd, follow_symlinks=False)
            if stat.S_ISLNK(entry_info.st_mode) or not stat.S_ISREG(entry_info.st_mode):
                continue
            if name in NON_CREDENTIAL_MARKERS:
                continue
            if not is_known_credential(name):
                unknown.append(name)
                continue
            data = _read_no_follow(name, fd)
            if not isinstance(data, bytes):
                continue  # vanished or became unreadable since the check above
            known.append((name, data))
        return known, unknown
    finally:
        os.close(fd)


def pending_against_dest(dest_dir: Path, known: list[tuple[str, bytes]]) -> list[tuple[str, bytes]]:
    """Which of `known` `dest_dir` does not yet hold exactly — missing,
    present with different bytes, or present as something (a symlink, a
    FIFO) this will not trust the content of either way.

    **Called only from inside a child already dropped to the reader
    account's own uid and gid.** Every destination read happens here,
    through `_read_dest`, which never blocks on a FIFO with no writer and
    never trusts anything but a regular file's content."""
    return [(name, data) for name, data in known if _read_dest(dest_dir / name) != data]


def _drop_privileges(uid: int, gid: int) -> None:
    """Become `(uid, gid)` in THIS process, with no supplementary groups
    carried over. Called only in a child that is about to do nothing else
    but read and write into that account's own home."""
    os.setgroups([])
    os.setgid(gid)
    os.setuid(uid)


def run_as_account(uid: int, gid: int, action, *, drop=None):
    """Run `action()` — a zero-argument callable returning a JSON-able
    value — in a child that has dropped to `(uid, gid)` first, and return
    what it returns, or raise `Refused` with what it raised.

    Modeled on `bin/install-jobs`'s own `as_account`, with one difference:
    that function's `action` reports success only as an exit code, and this
    one's callers — the crew-ops mint, the destination read and write —
    need to know WHAT was done, not just whether it crashed, so the result
    crosses the fork over a pipe rather than being thrown away. `drop` is
    injected so a test that cannot become another account can stub it, the
    same way `tools/test_install_jobs.py` already does for `as_account`.
    Looked up dynamically — `drop or _drop_privileges`, never a bound
    default — so a test that monkeypatches the module-level
    `_drop_privileges` itself reaches every caller that did not pass
    `drop=` explicitly, the same way the rest of this module's own
    monkeypatchable functions already work.
    """
    if drop is None:
        drop = _drop_privileges
    read_fd, write_fd = os.pipe()
    sys.stdout.flush()
    sys.stderr.flush()
    pid = os.fork()
    if pid == 0:
        os.close(read_fd)
        try:
            drop(uid, gid)
            result = action()
            payload = json.dumps({"ok": True, "result": result}).encode("utf-8")
        except BaseException as exc:
            payload = json.dumps({"ok": False, "error": str(exc)}).encode("utf-8")
        try:
            with os.fdopen(write_fd, "wb") as out:
                out.write(len(payload).to_bytes(8, "big"))
                out.write(payload)
        finally:
            os._exit(0)
    os.close(write_fd)
    with os.fdopen(read_fd, "rb") as inp:
        header = inp.read(8)
        size = int.from_bytes(header, "big") if header else 0
        data = inp.read(size) if size else b""
    os.waitpid(pid, 0)
    decoded = json.loads(data.decode("utf-8")) if data else {"ok": False, "error": "the child reported nothing"}
    if not decoded.get("ok"):
        raise Refused(decoded.get("error") or "the child failed silently")
    return decoded.get("result")


def _deliver_batch(dest_dir: Path, items: list[tuple[str, bytes]]) -> list[str]:
    """Write every `(name, data)` in `items` under `dest_dir`, mode 600,
    atomically. Runs with only the rights whoever calls this already has —
    see `run_as_account`, which is what drops to them first — so nothing
    here needs root and nothing here is trusted to have it.

    `dest_dir` itself is checked for a symlink first, the same refusal this
    always made. Each file is written to a `.delivering` tmp name opened
    with `O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_NOFOLLOW`, never through a link that could
    be planted at that exact name, then moved onto the real name with
    `os.replace` — a rename, which replaces whatever currently occupies
    that name, symlink or FIFO included, rather than writing through it, so
    the final name needs no symlink check of its own and the write itself
    never opens the real destination name at all.
    """
    if dest_dir.is_symlink() or (dest_dir.exists() and not dest_dir.is_dir()):
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing to use {dest_dir}: it is not a plain directory. A "
            f"symlink there would send a delivered credential wherever it "
            f"points. Nothing was delivered."
        )
    if not dest_dir.is_dir():
        dest_dir.mkdir(mode=0o700)
    delivered: list[str] = []
    for name, data in items:
        tmp = dest_dir / (name + ".delivering")
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass  # nothing stale to clear; the O_EXCL open below is the real guard
        fd = os.open(str(tmp), os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_WRONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW, 0o600)
        with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as out:
            out.write(data)
        os.replace(tmp, dest_dir / name)
        delivered.append(name)
    return delivered


def _plan_in_child(dest_dir: Path, known: list[tuple[str, bytes]]) -> list[str]:
    """What `run_as_account` runs, dropped to `agents`, for `--dry-run`,
    `--check` and `--status`: the names `agents` does not yet hold, read
    with its own rights. Writes nothing."""
    return [name for name, _ in pending_against_dest(dest_dir, known)]


def _deliver_in_child(dest_dir: Path, known: list[tuple[str, bytes]]) -> list[str]:
    """What `run_as_account` runs, dropped to `agents`, for a real
    delivery: read what is already there with its own rights, then write
    whatever differs, and return what was written."""
    pending = pending_against_dest(dest_dir, known)
    if not pending:
        return []
    return _deliver_batch(dest_dir, pending)


def deliver(
    known: list[tuple[str, bytes]],
    dest_dir: Path,
    uid: int,
    gid: int,
    *,
    dry_run: bool,
    run_as=run_as_account,
) -> list[str]:
    """Deliver everything in `known` that this destination does not already
    hold. Returns what WAS (or, under `dry_run`, WOULD BE) written; nothing
    is written under `dry_run`. Every read or write on the destination's
    side happens in the child `run_as` drops, never as root.

    `known` is computed once by the caller — `plan_delivery`, routed by
    `route_known` to whatever subset this one destination should receive —
    and passed in rather than recomputed here, so `source_dir` is never
    opened or read a second time per destination; see `plan_delivery`'s own
    docstring for why that matters."""
    child = _plan_in_child if dry_run else _deliver_in_child
    return run_as(uid, gid, lambda: child(dest_dir, known))


REGISTRY_READER_CREDENTIAL = "agent-platform-registry-reader-token"


def registry_reader_token_bytes(known: list[tuple[str, bytes]]) -> bytes | None:
    """The Library's own copy of `REGISTRY_READER_CREDENTIAL` out of `known`
    (`plan_delivery`'s own result) — the exact same bytes every entity's
    agents account already receives, since this is one of
    `KNOWN_CREDENTIAL_NAMES`'s entity-agnostic names. `None` when the
    Library has not minted it yet, the same "nothing has been minted
    anywhere this has not already looked" answer as every other credential
    this delivers."""
    for name, data in known:
        if name == REGISTRY_READER_CREDENTIAL:
            return data
    return None


def require_registry_reader_dir() -> Path:
    """`REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_DIR` as a plain directory, owned by `ROOT_UID`
    with mode exactly 0700 — `install-privileged`'s own `install_three`
    creates it at exactly that mode, directly under the prefix, before this
    ever runs. Raises `Refused`, naming the path, on anything else: missing,
    a symlink, not a directory, owned by some other uid, or a mode other
    than exactly 0700. This never creates the directory itself; a missing or
    wrongly-shaped one means the three-account root half has not finished
    installing, which is the operator's problem to fix, not this script's to
    paper over."""
    path = Path(REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_DIR)
    try:
        st = path.lstat()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        raise Refused(
            f"{path} does not exist. install-privileged's three-account root half "
            f"creates it; run that first. The registry reader token was not placed.")
    if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode) or not stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
        raise Refused(
            f"{path} is not a plain directory. The registry reader token was not placed.")
    if st.st_uid != ROOT_UID or stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode) != 0o700:
        raise Refused(
            f"{path} is owned by uid {st.st_uid} mode {oct(stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode))}, not "
            f"root-owned 0700. The registry reader token was not placed.")
    return path


def _write_registry_reader_token(dest_dir: Path, data: bytes) -> None:
    """Write `data` to `dest_dir`/`REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_NAME`, mode 600,
    atomically — the same discipline `_deliver_batch` uses for an agents
    home: a `.delivering` tmp name opened with `O_CREAT | O_EXCL |
    O_NOFOLLOW`, never through a link planted at that exact name, then moved
    onto the real name with `os.replace`, which replaces whatever currently
    occupies it, symlink or FIFO included, rather than writing through it."""
    tmp = dest_dir / (REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_NAME + ".delivering")
    try:
        os.unlink(tmp)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass  # nothing stale to clear; the O_EXCL open below is the real guard
    fd = os.open(str(tmp), os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_WRONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as out:
        os.fchown(out.fileno(), ROOT_UID, ROOT_GID)
        out.write(data)
    os.replace(tmp, dest_dir / REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_NAME)


def deliver_registry_reader_token(known: list[tuple[str, bytes]], *, dry_run: bool) -> str | None:
    """Deliver the Library's own copy of `REGISTRY_READER_CREDENTIAL` to
    `REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_DIR`/`REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_NAME` — the three-account
    shape's own root-owned destination for it, never any account's
    `.orient-control`. Returns `REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_NAME` when it was (or,
    under `dry_run`, would be) written, `None` when the Library has not
    minted the credential yet or the destination already holds the exact
    same bytes. Raises `Refused` — from `require_registry_reader_dir` — when
    there is something to deliver and the destination directory is missing
    or not root-owned 0700; nothing is checked or written when there is
    nothing to deliver."""
    data = registry_reader_token_bytes(known)
    if data is None:
        return None
    dest_dir = require_registry_reader_dir()
    if _read_dest(dest_dir / REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_NAME) == data:
        return None
    if not dry_run:
        _write_registry_reader_token(dest_dir, data)
    return REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_NAME


def mint_missing_crew_credentials(
    library_home: Path, uid: int, gid: int, *,
    script: Path | None = None, run_as=run_as_account, runner=subprocess.run,
) -> str | None:
    """Mint the ops-credential pair for any crew registered since the last
    time this ran, directly under the Library account's own
    `~/.orient-control` — so a `deliver()` right after this can copy it to
    `agents`.

    **WHY THIS RUNS HERE AND NOT WHERE THE CREW WAS REGISTERED.**
    `mint-crew-ops-credentials` reads the registry from the Library's own
    database directly (its own module docstring says why: the Library may
    not be serving yet, the first time it runs). On the shapes this
    delivers on, that database lives under the Library account's home,
    mode 700 — a crew's own session, running as `agents`, cannot open it,
    so `new-crew`'s own plain invocation of that command fails there. This
    runs the same command instead, as the Library's own account, from the
    privileged process that already reads that account's home for
    `deliver()`'s own sake.

    Returns `None` on success, including "nothing to mint" — or a message
    to report when the mint step itself failed, including when `script`
    does not exist: this ships in the same tree as this tool (see
    `DEFAULT_CREW_MINT_SCRIPT`), so a missing script is never a sign of an
    older release, only a broken install, and silently skipping it would
    report success while leaving a new crew with no credentials. Delivery
    still proceeds with whatever was already minted either way: a crew's
    missing ops pair is not a reason to withhold credentials every other
    crew already has.
    """
    script = script or DEFAULT_CREW_MINT_SCRIPT
    if not script.is_file():
        return f"{script} does not exist; this release's own copy is missing"
    state = library_home / ".local" / "state" / "library"
    deploy = library_home / ".local" / "deploy" / "library"
    orient_control = library_home / ORIENT_CONTROL

    def run_it() -> int:
        result = runner(
            [sys.executable, str(script),
             "--library-db", str(state / "library.db"),
             "--tokens-file", str(state / "api-tokens.json"),
             "--library-dir", str(deploy),
             "--orient-control", str(orient_control)],
            check=False,
        )
        return result.returncode

    try:
        returncode = run_as(uid, gid, run_it)
    except Refused as exc:
        return str(exc)
    if returncode != 0:
        return f"mint-crew-ops-credentials exited {returncode}; see its own output above"
    return None


def destination_accounts(install_json: str) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """Every `(entity, agents-account-name)` pair a credential this
    recognises might be delivered to: `entity` is the install's own entity
    slug (`"default"` when `install_json` names none), and the account name
    is the bare `agents` every install used before entities existed, or
    each entity's own `<entity>-agents` — read from `install_json` the
    same way `provision-accounts` and `install-privileged` already read
    it. Raises `Refused` naming the file on an unreadable or malformed
    entities list.

    An entity-agnostic credential (everything `crew_candidates_for_ops_name`
    returns `[]` for) goes to every one of these; a crew's own pair goes
    only to the one whose `entity` its registry row names — see
    `route_known`."""
    try:
        entities = ae.read_entities(install_json)
    except ae.EntitiesInvalid as exc:
        raise Refused(str(exc))
    pairs = ae.account_pairs(entities)
    return [(entity, agents) for entity, (agents, _reviewers) in zip(entities, pairs)]


def read_install_shape(install_json: str) -> str | None:
    """The `shape` field `install-privileged` wrote to `install_json`, or
    `None` when the file is absent, unreadable, not JSON, not an object, or
    names no `shape` at all. Duplicated from `bin/uninstall-builder`'s own
    `read_install_shape` rather than imported, the same way every other
    constant this file needs from a sibling installer is duplicated.

    **NEVER RAISES.** The caller treats every one of those cases as "not the
    three-account shape" — the single-account and two-account shapes have no
    `reviewers` account and no review launcher to serve, so they read the
    same as a shape this cannot determine yet."""
    try:
        text = Path(install_json).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        return None
    try:
        data = json.loads(text)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        return None
    shape = data.get("shape")
    return shape if isinstance(shape, str) else None


def _crew_entities_from_db(db_path: Path) -> dict[str, str]:
    """Every crew the registry carries, mapped to the `entity` named on its
    own row — read the `project_registry` table the same way
    `payload/bin/mint-crew-ops-credentials`'s own `registered_crews` reads
    it, reproduced here rather than imported, for the same reason that
    module's own docstring gives: this must not depend on a change to the
    Library to run.

    Returns `{}` when the database or its table does not exist yet —
    nothing has been registered anywhere this has not already looked. A
    row naming no `entity` at all, or one whose `entry` is not valid JSON
    or not an object, contributes nothing rather than refusing — the same
    "report, do not guess" choice `route_known` makes for a crew this
    mapping has nothing for."""
    if not db_path.is_file():
        return {}
    try:
        conn = sqlite3.connect(str(db_path))
    except sqlite3.Error:
        return {}
    try:
        try:
            rows = conn.execute("SELECT slug, entry FROM project_registry").fetchall()
        except sqlite3.OperationalError:
            return {}
    finally:
        conn.close()
    mapping: dict[str, str] = {}
    for slug, entry_text in rows:
        if slug.startswith("_"):
            continue
        try:
            entry = json.loads(entry_text)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if not isinstance(entry, dict):
            continue
        crew = entry.get("crew")
        crew = crew if isinstance(crew, str) and crew else slug
        entity = entry.get("entity")
        if isinstance(entity, str) and entity:
            mapping[crew] = entity
    return mapping


def crew_entity_map(
    library_home: Path, uid: int, gid: int, *, run_as=run_as_account, reader=_crew_entities_from_db,
) -> dict[str, str]:
    """Every registered crew mapped to its own entity, read as the
    Library's own account — in a child dropped to `(uid, gid)`, the same
    way `mint_missing_crew_credentials` runs its own mint step, so root
    never opens the Library's database at all. `reader` is injected so a
    test that cannot become another account can stub it, the same way
    `mint_missing_crew_credentials`' own `runner` already is.

    Called only on a multi-entity install — see the module docstring's "A
    single-entity install... does not need the mapping."""
    db_path = library_home / ".local" / "state" / "library" / "library.db"
    return run_as(uid, gid, lambda: reader(db_path))


def _registered_crews_from_db(db_path: Path) -> list[str]:
    """Every crew the registry carries, whether or not its row names an
    entity, read the same way `_crew_entities_from_db` reads it. `[]` when
    the database or its table does not exist yet."""
    if not db_path.is_file():
        return []
    try:
        conn = sqlite3.connect(str(db_path))
    except sqlite3.Error:
        return []
    try:
        try:
            rows = conn.execute("SELECT slug, entry FROM project_registry").fetchall()
        except sqlite3.OperationalError:
            return []
    finally:
        conn.close()
    crews: list[str] = []
    for slug, entry_text in rows:
        if slug.startswith("_"):
            continue
        try:
            entry = json.loads(entry_text)
        except ValueError:
            entry = None
        crew = entry.get("crew") if isinstance(entry, dict) else None
        crews.append(crew if isinstance(crew, str) and crew else slug)
    return sorted(set(crews))


def registered_crew_names(
    library_home: Path, uid: int, gid: int, *, run_as=run_as_account, reader=_registered_crews_from_db,
) -> list[str]:
    """Every registered crew, read as the Library's own account the same
    way `crew_entity_map` reads. Read on every install shape, because a
    crew `<x>` and a crew `co-<x>` share `ops-co-<x>-token` however many
    agents accounts there are: one crew's session would read a credential
    carrying the other's grant, the `item.create` grant included."""
    db_path = library_home / ".local" / "state" / "library" / "library.db"
    return run_as(uid, gid, lambda: reader(db_path))


def route_known(
    known: list[tuple[str, bytes]],
    destinations: list[tuple[str, str]],
    crew_entities: dict[str, str] | None,
    shared=(),
) -> tuple[dict[str, list[tuple[str, bytes]]], list[str]]:
    """Split `known` into what each destination in `destinations` (an
    `(entity, agents-account-name)` pair from `destination_accounts`)
    should receive, and the crew-scoped names that could not be routed to
    any of them.

    Every entity-agnostic name (`crew_candidates_for_ops_name` returns
    `[]`) goes to every destination, exactly as before this routing
    existed. A crew-scoped name is resolved against `crew_entities` by
    EVERY candidate `crew_candidates_for_ops_name` returns for it — more
    than one when a leading `co-` makes the file name ambiguous between
    two different crews' own pairs (see that function's own docstring).
    Exactly one candidate naming a registered crew routes to that crew's
    entity. Both candidates registered, under the same entity or different
    ones, or neither registered at all, reports the name back rather than
    guessing between them or delivering it to every destination. When both
    are registered the file is one `mint-crew-ops-credentials` now refuses
    to mint, so a copy can only predate that refusal and may hold either
    crew's credential. This is the same "report, do not guess" choice as a
    single unambiguous crew with no entity; see the module docstring.

    `shared` names every file two registered crews could own
    (`shared_ops_names`, from every registered crew whether or not its row
    names an entity). Each is reported and delivered nowhere, on every
    install shape, before any other rule is read.

    `crew_entities` is `None` on the single-entity install, where there is
    exactly one destination and every name outside `shared` reaches it;
    the entity mapping is never read in that case.
    """
    shared = set(shared)
    by_name: dict[str, list[tuple[str, bytes]]] = {name: [] for _entity, name in destinations}
    orphaned: list[str] = []
    if crew_entities is None:
        for item in known:
            if item[0] in shared:
                orphaned.append(item[0])
                continue
            for items in by_name.values():
                items.append(item)
        return by_name, orphaned
    account_for_entity = {entity: name for entity, name in destinations}
    for item in known:
        name, _data = item
        if name in shared:
            orphaned.append(name)
            continue
        candidates = crew_candidates_for_ops_name(name)
        if not candidates:
            for items in by_name.values():
                items.append(item)
            continue
        registered = [c for c in candidates if c in crew_entities]
        dest_name = (account_for_entity.get(crew_entities[registered[0]])
                     if len(registered) == 1 else None)
        if dest_name is None:
            orphaned.append(name)
            continue
        by_name[dest_name].append(item)
    return by_name, orphaned


def shared_ops_names(crews) -> list[str]:
    """Every crew-ops file name two registered crews could both own: a crew
    `<x>` and a crew `co-<x>` both read `ops-co-<x>-token`, the first as
    its `item.create` pair member and the second as its plain one.
    `route_known` delivers such a name to nobody; this names them from the
    registry alone, so a copy delivered before the second crew registered
    is found and revoked even after the operator deletes the Library's own
    source file, as `mint-crew-ops-credentials`' refusal tells them to.
    `crews` is every registered crew name (`registered_crew_names`), on
    every install shape."""
    crews = set(crews)
    names = []
    for crew in crews:
        name = f"ops-co-{crew}-token"
        if f"co-{crew}" in crews and CREW_OPS_RE.match(name):
            names.append(name)
    return sorted(names)


def _revoke_in_child(dest_dir: Path, names: list[str], dry_run: bool) -> list[str]:
    """What `run_as_account` runs, dropped to a destination's own account:
    remove each of `names` that `dest_dir` holds, and return what was (or,
    under `dry_run`, would be) removed. A copy of a shared crew-ops name may
    hold either crew's credential, so the account keeps none.

    `dest_dir` is opened once with `O_NOFOLLOW | O_DIRECTORY` and every
    lookup and unlink is relative to that descriptor. An unlink removes
    whatever sits at the name, a symlink included, and never its target. A
    missing `dest_dir` holds nothing to revoke; one that is not a plain
    directory is refused, the same as `_deliver_batch` refuses it."""
    if not names:
        return []
    try:
        fd = os.open(str(dest_dir), DIR_OPEN_FLAGS)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return []
    except OSError as exc:
        raise Refused(
            f"refusing to revoke from {dest_dir}: it is not a plain directory ({exc}). "
            f"Nothing was revoked there."
        )
    removed: list[str] = []
    try:
        for name in names:
            try:
                os.stat(name, dir_fd=fd, follow_symlinks=False)
            except FileNotFoundError:
                continue
            if not dry_run:
                try:
                    os.unlink(name, dir_fd=fd)
                except FileNotFoundError:
                    continue
            removed.append(name)
    finally:
        os.close(fd)
    return removed


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog=PROG,
        description="Deliver the Library's minted credentials to the accounts that use them.",
    )
    mode = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    mode.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true", help="say what would happen, change nothing")
    mode.add_argument("--check", action="store_true",
                      help="report only; 1 if anything is still undelivered")
    mode.add_argument("--status", action="store_true", help="the same report, changing nothing")
    parser.add_argument("--install-json", default=ae.DEFAULT_INSTALL_JSON,
                        help="the install's record, read for the entities each destination "
                        f"account is named after (default: {ae.DEFAULT_INSTALL_JSON})")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    if os.geteuid() != 0:
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — this needs root, to read the Library's account home and "
              f"reach its destinations'. Nothing was delivered.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    library_name = library_account_name()
    library_home_str = account_home(library_name)
    if library_home_str is None:
        print(f"{PROG}: no {library_name} account on this machine — nothing to deliver. "
              f"Either this is the single-account shape, where the Library runs as the "
              f"operator, or the Library's own account has not been provisioned yet.")
        return 0

    try:
        destinations = destination_accounts(args.install_json)
    except Refused as exc:
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    three_account = read_install_shape(args.install_json) == "three"

    library_ids = account_ids(library_name)
    assert library_ids is not None  # just resolved by name above
    if not owned_by(library_home_str, library_ids[0]):
        owner = os.lstat(library_home_str).st_uid
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — {library_home_str} (home of {library_name}) is owned by uid "
              f"{owner}, not its own account. Something else made it; this will not read "
              f"from it. Nothing was delivered.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    orient_name = orient_account_name()
    orient_home_str = account_home(orient_name)
    missing = [name for _entity, name in destinations if account_home(name) is None]
    if orient_home_str is None:
        missing.append(orient_name)
    if missing:
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — {library_name} exists but {', '.join(missing)} does not. "
              f"Run provision-accounts with this shape first. Nothing was delivered.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    dest_infos: list[tuple[str, Path, int, int]] = []
    for _entity, name in destinations:
        home_str = account_home(name)
        assert home_str is not None  # just checked by `missing` above
        uid, gid = account_ids(name)
        if not owned_by(home_str, uid):
            owner = os.lstat(home_str).st_uid
            print(f"{PROG}: refusing — {home_str} (home of {name}) is owned by uid "
                  f"{owner}, not its own account. Something else made it; this will not write "
                  f"into it. Nothing was delivered.", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        dest_infos.append((name, Path(home_str) / ORIENT_CONTROL, uid, gid))

    orient_ids = account_ids(orient_name)
    assert orient_ids is not None  # just checked by `missing` above
    if not owned_by(orient_home_str, orient_ids[0]):
        owner = os.lstat(orient_home_str).st_uid
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — {orient_home_str} (home of {orient_name}) is owned by uid "
              f"{owner}, not its own account. Something else made it; this will not write "
              f"into it. Nothing was delivered.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    orient_dest_dir = Path(orient_home_str) / ORIENT_CONTROL

    library_home = Path(library_home_str)
    source_dir = library_home / ORIENT_CONTROL
    crew_entities = crew_entity_map(library_home, *library_ids) if len(destinations) > 1 else None
    shared = shared_ops_names(registered_crew_names(library_home, *library_ids))

    def revoke_shared(dry_run: bool) -> list[tuple[str, Path]]:
        """Each `(name, dest_dir)` a shared crew-ops name was (or would be)
        removed from, every destination read and written as its own
        account."""
        revoked: list[tuple[str, Path]] = []
        for _name, dest_dir, uid, gid in dest_infos:
            names = run_as_account(
                uid, gid, lambda dest_dir=dest_dir: _revoke_in_child(dest_dir, shared, dry_run))
            revoked.extend((rname, dest_dir) for rname in names)
        return revoked

    def plan_and_route() -> tuple[
        dict[str, list[tuple[str, bytes]]], list[str], list[str],
        list[tuple[str, bytes]], list[tuple[str, bytes]],
    ]:
        """`plan_delivery` and `route_known`, run together exactly once —
        never per destination; see `plan_delivery`'s own docstring. `known`
        itself is also returned, unrouted, for `deliver_registry_reader_token`
        — the three-account shape's own root destination is nobody's entity,
        so it is never one of `route_known`'s own per-destination lists.

        Orient's own three credentials (`ORIENT_CREDENTIAL_NAMES`) are split
        out of `known` BEFORE `route_known` ever sees it, and returned
        separately as `orient_known`: Orient is one account regardless of
        how many entities this install declares, so none of the three is
        ever one of `route_known`'s own per-entity destinations either."""
        known, unknown = plan_delivery(source_dir, library_ids[0])
        orient_known = [item for item in known if item[0] in ORIENT_CREDENTIAL_NAMES]
        rest_known = [item for item in known if item[0] not in ORIENT_CREDENTIAL_NAMES]
        routed, orphaned = route_known(rest_known, destinations, crew_entities, shared)
        return routed, unknown, orphaned, known, orient_known

    def report_orphaned(orphaned: list[str]) -> None:
        if orphaned:
            print(f"{PROG}: {len(orphaned)} crew ops credential(s) could not be routed to any "
                  f"entity's own agents account: {', '.join(orphaned)}. Delivered to nobody. "
                  f"Each is either a name two registered crews could own (a crew <x> and a "
                  f"crew co-<x> both read ops-co-<x>-token), in which case rename one crew and "
                  f"delete the Library's own copy of the shared file (a real run removes every "
                  f"delivered copy), or a name whose crew has no entity on its registry "
                  f"row or names one this install does not declare.", file=sys.stderr)

    if args.check or args.status:
        try:
            routed, unknown, orphaned, known, orient_known = plan_and_route()
        except Refused as exc:
            print(f"{PROG}: refusing — {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        report_orphaned(orphaned)
        pending: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
        for name, dest_dir, uid, gid in dest_infos:
            pending_names = run_as_account(
                uid, gid, lambda dest_dir=dest_dir, items=routed[name]: _plan_in_child(dest_dir, items))
            pending.extend((pname, name) for pname in pending_names)
        orient_pending_names = run_as_account(
            orient_ids[0], orient_ids[1],
            lambda: _plan_in_child(orient_dest_dir, orient_known))
        pending.extend((pname, orient_name) for pname in orient_pending_names)
        if three_account:
            try:
                root_pending = deliver_registry_reader_token(known, dry_run=True)
            except Refused as exc:
                print(f"{PROG}: refusing — {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
            if root_pending:
                pending.append((root_pending, "root"))
        if pending:
            described = ", ".join(f"{pname} (to {dname})" for pname, dname in pending)
            print(f"{PROG}: {len(pending)} credential(s) not yet delivered: {described}")
        else:
            print(f"{PROG}: every credential the Library has minted is delivered")
        if unknown:
            print(f"{PROG}: not something this knows how to deliver, so never delivered: "
                  f"{', '.join(unknown)}")
        try:
            held = revoke_shared(dry_run=True)
        except Refused as exc:
            print(f"{PROG}: refusing — {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        if held:
            described = ", ".join(str(dest_dir / rname) for rname, dest_dir in held)
            print(f"{PROG}: {len(held)} copy(ies) of a crew ops credential two registered "
                  f"crews could own, which a real run removes: {described}", file=sys.stderr)
        exit_nonzero = (args.check and (pending or held)) or (orphaned and not args.status)
        return 1 if exit_nonzero else 0

    try:
        if not args.dry_run:
            mint_problem = mint_missing_crew_credentials(library_home, library_ids[0], library_ids[1])
            if mint_problem:
                print(f"{PROG}: minting a newly registered crew's ops credentials failed: "
                      f"{mint_problem}. Delivering what is already minted anyway.",
                      file=sys.stderr)
        # Planned AFTER minting, so a crew registered since the last run —
        # whose pair the mint step just wrote above — is read and delivered
        # in this same call; see mint_missing_crew_credentials's own
        # docstring.
        routed, unknown, orphaned, known, orient_known = plan_and_route()
        report_orphaned(orphaned)
        verb = "would deliver" if args.dry_run else "delivered"
        any_delivered = False
        for name, dest_dir, uid, gid in dest_infos:
            delivered = deliver(routed[name], dest_dir, uid, gid, dry_run=args.dry_run)
            for cred_name in delivered:
                any_delivered = True
                print(f"{PROG}: {verb} {cred_name} to {dest_dir / cred_name}")
        orient_delivered = deliver(orient_known, orient_dest_dir, orient_ids[0], orient_ids[1],
                                   dry_run=args.dry_run)
        for cred_name in orient_delivered:
            any_delivered = True
            print(f"{PROG}: {verb} {cred_name} to {orient_dest_dir / cred_name}")
        if three_account:
            root_delivered = deliver_registry_reader_token(known, dry_run=args.dry_run)
            if root_delivered:
                any_delivered = True
                root_path = Path(REGISTRY_READER_ROOT_DIR) / root_delivered
                print(f"{PROG}: {verb} {root_delivered} to {root_path}")
        revoke_verb = "would revoke" if args.dry_run else "revoked"
        for rname, dest_dir in revoke_shared(dry_run=args.dry_run):
            print(f"{PROG}: {revoke_verb} {dest_dir / rname}: two registered crews could own "
                  f"that name, so the copy may hold either crew's credential")
    except Refused as exc:
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    if not any_delivered:
        destination_names = ", ".join([*(name for _entity, name in destinations), orient_name])
        print(f"{PROG}: nothing to deliver — {destination_names} already has every "
              f"credential the Library has minted")
    if unknown:
        print(f"{PROG}: not something this knows how to deliver, so never delivered: "
              f"{', '.join(unknown)}")
    # A REAL RUN (`--dry-run` INCLUDED) EXITS 0 EVEN WHEN SOMETHING WAS
    # ORPHANED. `report_orphaned` above already put it on stderr, and
    # everything else was still delivered. `bin/stand-up-backend`'s own step
    # loop stops a REAL run on ANY non-zero code, not only a refusal, so a
    # `1` here would abort the whole install — library-person and
    # knowledge-index included — over one crew's stale or misconfigured
    # registry row, while every credential that row has nothing to do with
    # already reached its destination. Nothing here remembers that a name
    # was orphaned, so the next run reads the same registry row and reports
    # it again. `--check`, above, still exits 1 for it: that mode's whole
    # job is to say whether work is left.
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
